{"id": "sevt_016W4soToJSVHwW6vvazSaN8", "content": [{"text": "No, auth isn't the problem. The cause is the checkout deploy `a3f9c21`.\n\n- **Timing:** `a3f9c21` deployed to checkout at 14:31:18. Checkout p99 jumped from about 75 ms to about 3,860 ms at 14:31 and has climbed to about 4,900 ms since. The gap is under a minute.\n- **Diff:** The \"readability refactor\" of `build_summary` replaced one batched query with a per-order loop. The old code ran a single `order_id__in` query and grouped the results in memory. The new code runs `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id=...)` once for every open order (an N+1 pattern).\n- **Log:** There are 3,320 per-order `SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id = ?` queries, starting at 14:31:18.496, which is the deploy second. Several of them take 20 ms or more.\n- **Auth:** Auth p99 stayed flat at about 50\u201380 ms through the whole window. Its last deploy, `9f1e2d4`, was at 11:02, well before the incident. The auth log does show identity-provider timeouts at 14:15. That is 16 minutes before checkout degraded, and auth latency never moved, so they don't explain this.\n- **Other deploys:** The cart (09:14) and inventory (14:55) deploys don't line up with the start of the spike. The inventory deploy came 24 minutes after it began.\n- **Fix:** Roll back `a3f9c21`, or restore the batched `order_id__in` query with in-memory grouping. I haven't checked database pool utilization.\n\n**Root cause:** `a3f9c21` \u2014 the checkout order-summary refactor replaced one batched `order_items` query with a per-order query in a loop (N+1), so each checkout summary issues many sequential DB round-trips and p99 rose from about 75 ms to about 4 s.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T11:45:06.741475Z", "type": "agent.message"}
